WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.640
A hawk's shadow slides across a meadow.

2
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.080
What does a mouse's optic nerve tell its brain about it?

3
00:00:10.033 --> 00:00:14.093
Every signal the brain receives from the eye leaves through ganglion cells.

4
00:00:15.133 --> 00:00:19.853
They are the retina's only output neurons, and their axons make the optic nerve.

5
00:00:20.867 --> 00:00:23.967
So does that nerve carry a picture, like a camera cable?

6
00:00:25.017 --> 00:00:29.477
It does not. A mouse has more than 40 types of ganglion cell.

7
00:00:30.467 --> 00:00:32.987
Each type reports the scene through its own filter.

8
00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:35.407
Some filters are simple.

9
00:00:36.307 --> 00:00:38.247
They signal local differences in brightness.

10
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.020
Others are highly selective.

11
00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:45.360
They detect a small object moving, or motion in one direction.

12
00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:51.710
Bipolar and amacrine circuits inside the retina build each filter, before any spike

13
00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:52.710
leaves the eye.

14
00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:57.300
Now look down on the retina as a flat sheet, and light up one type.

15
00:00:58.233 --> 00:01:00.113
Its cells spread across the retina.

16
00:01:01.053 --> 00:01:03.513
Each cell tends to keep some distance from its neighbours.

17
00:01:04.667 --> 00:01:09.407
Their dendrites can overlap a lot, and spacing and arbor size vary across the

18
00:01:09.407 --> 00:01:10.407
retina.

19
00:01:10.717 --> 00:01:12.937
That even spacing is called a mosaic.

20
00:01:13.697 --> 00:01:15.697
So one type draws one map of the scene.

21
00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.540
Lay a second type over the first.

22
00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:20.460
Then a third.

23
00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:25.840
Predict. If each type draws one map, what does the optic nerve carry?

24
00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:35.820
More than 40 overlapping maps of the scene, each filtered differently.

25
00:01:36.883 --> 00:01:39.343
The brain can combine maps to build new features.

26
00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:44.533
But information that no map carries is gone before the brain has a chance.

27
00:01:45.583 --> 00:01:47.443
The same idea suggests a check.

28
00:01:48.223 --> 00:01:51.863
If a type is really two, its spacing usually looks less regular.

29
00:01:52.917 --> 00:01:56.317
And more arbors cover each point than for comparable types.

30
00:01:57.137 --> 00:01:58.297
These are clues, not proof.

31
00:01:59.317 --> 00:02:00.797
Hold one rule in mind.

32
00:02:01.617 --> 00:02:03.397
One type, one mosaic, one map.
